to know:\\n\\n- Investors yanked over $1 bi...\"\n  ],\n  \"aggregated_raw_content\": \"# HYPE funds attract millions as investors dump bitcoin and ether ETFs\\n\\n## Investors turn to HYPE and XRP funds while dumping bitcoin and ether ETFs.\\n\\n#### What to know:\\n\\n- Investors yanked over $1 billion from bitcoin ETFs last week and over $215 million from ether funds, signaling waning appetite for broad large-cap crypto exposure.\\n- Instead, the capital is rotating into select altcoins, with new HYPE spot products drawing about $72 million and XRP and SOL ETFs attracting $22 million and $15.6 million, respectively.\\n- Hyperliquid’s HYPE token has surged 59% this month amid rising fees and increasing trading volumes in RWA-linked markets on the platform.\\n\\nCrypto fund flows are starting to fracture, with investors exiting bitcoin\\n\\nBitcoin ETFs saw more than $1 billion in outflows last week, extending a sharp institutional pullback, while ether funds lost another $215 million, according to data source SoSoValue. The continued bleeding from the two largest assets signals a cooling appetite for broad, benchmark crypto exposure.\\n\\nBut the redemptions haven't been uniform.\\n\\nSpot products investing in Hyperliquid’s hype token, issued by Bitwise and 21Shares, attracted a combined $72.38 million, underscoring that capital is being redeployed with precision rather than exiting the market altogether. XRP and sol ETFs registered inflows worth $22 million and $15.6 million, respectively.\\n\\n\\\"The broader message: capital has not left crypto uniformly. It is rotating toward newer narratives and away from crowded large-cap exposure,\\\" Timothy Misir, head of research at BRN, said in an email.\\n\\n## Hype is real\\n\\nThe strong uptake for hype ETFs, which went live a week ago, coincides with a sharp rally in the token’s price and robust network activity.\\n\\nThe token has been on a tear, jumping from $38 to $63 in the past 10 days, CoinDesk data show. It has gained 59% for the month, a staggering performance compared with market leader bitcoin’s 1% gain.\\n\\nDecentralized platform Hyperliquid has generated $13.2 million in fees over the past seven days, the fifth-largest tally, trailing stablecoin behemoths such as Tether and Circle Internet (CRCL) as well as launchpad Pump. Canton Network ranks fourth, though, according to DeFiLlama, that is largely driven by substantial incentives.\\n\\nHyperliquid's revenue is expected to rise further, thanks to its recent agreement with Coinbase and Circle to integrate stablecoin USDC as a quote asset.\\n\\nSome analysts say Hyperliquid is rapidly emerging as a challenger to traditional trading platforms and prediction markets. And for good reasons: Since the Iran war began in late February, the platform's HIP-3 market has consistently handled millions in trading volume in perpetual futures tied to traditional and real-world assets (RWA) such as oil, gold and U.S. equity indexes.\\n\\n\\\"Hyperliquid fundamental metrics continue to strengthen across the board as HIP-3 markets reached new weekly highs at 2.6B in open interest across RWA perp markets. HIP-4 launched outcome markets a couple of weeks ago to more modest growth,\\\" data tracking website Artemis said in the weekly newsletter.\\n\\n\\\"Equity perpetuals, pre-IPO markets and prediction markets are all in the very early innings, and Hyperliquid is well positioned to capitalize on that momentum,\\\" Artemis said.\\n\\nMore For You\\n\\nA new report shows Hyperliquid is rapidly expanding beyond crypto into pre-IPO markets, prediction contracts, and 24/7 asset trading, putting Wall Street giants on high alert.\\n\\nWhat to know:\\n\\n- Crypto derivatives platform Hyperliquid is expanding beyond perpetual futures into pre-IPO trading, prediction contracts and tokenized real-world assets, putting it in more direct competition with traditional exchanges and prediction markets.\\n- FalconX said Hyperliquid’s HIP-3 and HIP-4 markets, along with strong inflows into new HYPE exchange-traded funds and a USDC partnership...\"\n}"}],"isError":false,"timestamp":1779705869962}}
{"type":"message","id":"ae5e09b1","parentId":"7a3de415","timestamp":"2026-05-25T10:44:29.962Z","message":{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"read","toolName":"read","content":[{"type":"text","text":"# COINOGRAPHY EDITORIAL RULES\n\nFollow these rules for every article. Interior structure (how many sections, headings, hook style) is your editorial decision within the borders below.\n\n---\n\n## Hard rules (never break)\n\n**Content quality**\n- **NO EM-DASHES:** Never use `—`. Use a comma, period, or restructure.\n- **NATURAL WRITING:** Never use: \"it's worth noting\", \"it is important to note\", \"delve into\", \"in conclusion\", \"furthermore\", \"moreover\", \"in summary\", \"the crypto landscape\", \"the world of crypto\", \"a testament to\", \"shed light on\".\n- **TONE:** Direct. Trader-focused. Analytical. No hype or fluff (\"amazing\", \"shocking\").\n- **PARAGRAPHS:** 1–4 sentences max. One idea per paragraph.\n- **BULLET LISTS:** 3–4 items only, inside H3 sections if you use them. Never in Conclusion or opening hook.\n\n**SEO and links**\n- **ANCHOR LINKS:** Exactly **2** markdown links to URLs from `RESEARCH_JSON.source_urls` (distinct URLs, no repeats). Both in the **opening hook or first H2 only**. No x.com or twitter.com links in the body.\n- **KEYWORDS:** Primary keyword in H1 (first 5 words); in first 100 words; in at least one H2 and in Conclusion. Use secondary keywords in H2/H3 where natural.\n\n**Length**\n- **Word count:** Body **1000–1200** words (aim **1100**). Count body only; META, Sources, and `[Word Count:]` line are not counted.\n\n**Fixed section order**\n```\nMETA → H1 (hook) → H2 body sections → ## Conclusion → ## FAQs → **Sources:** → [Word Count: NNNN]\n```\n- **## Conclusion** must appear immediately before **## FAQs**.\n- **## FAQs** must be the **last** section in the content body (immediately before **Sources:**).\n- Do not put FAQs before Conclusion or interleave FAQs in the body.\n\n---\n\n## Structure borders (you decide inside these limits)\n\n| Element | Minimum | Maximum |\n|---------|---------|---------|\n| H2 body sections (excluding Conclusion, FAQs) | 2 | 4 |\n| H3 subsections (`###`) total | 3 | 6 |\n| FAQ items under `## FAQs` | 3 | 6 |\n\nWithin these borders, you choose:\n- How many H2s, H3s, and FAQs to use\n- Heading text and angle for each section\n- How to open the article (2–4 paragraphs under H1)\n- Section depth, bullet lists, and flow\n\nThere is **no** prescribed mapping from fact count to section count. Decide what structure best serves **this** story.\n\nEach H3 should draw on distinct material from research; do not pad with empty sections.\n\n---\n\n## META block (required first)\n\nThe first word of the file must be **META**.\n\nCount characters in `<thinking>` before writing:\n\n| Field | Your max | Rank Math cap |\n|-------|----------|---------------|\n| SEO Title | **55** | 60 |\n| Meta Description | **155** | 160 |\n| URL Slug | **70** | 75 |\n\n```\nMETA\n- SEO Title: [primary keyword early; add | Coinography only if ≤55 chars total]\n- Meta Description: [hook + detail; ≤155 chars]\n- URL Slug: [lowercase hyphens; 3–5 words; ≤70 chars]\n- Categories: [e.g. Regulation, Latest News]\n- Primary Keyword: [...]\n- Secondary Keywords: [...]\n```\n\n---\n\n## H1 and body\n\nAfter META and `---`, write **one** H1:\n\n`# [Primary keyword in first 5 words; include a price or number when relevant]`\n\nThen your hook and H2/H3 body sections. Heading names and section count are your choice within the borders above.\n\n---\n\n## Conclusion (required)\n\n`## Conclusion`\n\nRestate the event with primary keyword and actionable close for traders. Length is your call within the total word band.\n\n---\n\n## FAQs (required, last before Sources)\n\n`## FAQs`\n\nWrite questions that fit **this** story (no fixed question bank). **3–6** items within the FAQ border.\n\nFormat each item:\n```\n**1. Your question here?**\n\nAnswer paragraph, 3–4 sentences.\n```\n\nRenumber 1..N. Do not use markdown list syntax for questions.\n\n---\n\n## Sources footer (required)\n\nDo not use `## Sources` or `## Word Count` as headings.\n\n```\n**Sources:**\n- [Publication]: [URL]\n- [Publication]: [URL]\n\n[Word Count: 1100]\n```\n\nFinal line: `[Word Count: NNNN]` with digits only.\n\n---\n\n## Pre-flight (in `<thinking>`)\n\n- [ ] META char counts within limits\n- [ ] 2 source URLs planned for hook/first H2\n- [ ] Structure choices within H2/H3/FAQ borders\n- [ ] Body word budget 1000–1200\n- [ ] Order: Conclusion then FAQs then Sources\n\n## Post-output check\n\n- [ ] 1 H1; H2/H3/FAQ counts within borders\n- [ ] Conclusion before FAQs; FAQs before Sources\n- [ ] 1000–1200 body words\n- [ ] Exactly 2 source links in hook/first H2\n- [ ] No em-dashes or banned phrases\n"}],"isError":false,"timestamp":1779705869962}}
{"type":"message","id":"fb93be46","parentId":"ae5e09b1","timestamp":"2026-05-25T10:44:32.305Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"toolCall","id":"exec_ts_ZTI0ODMwYTctNWNkNi00MmZlLTk5OGItZWU1MzllNzJiOWMz","name":"exec","arguments":{"command":"cat /tmp/crypto-run-20260525-103551/article/raw.md"}}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"local-bifrost","model":"gemini-3.1-pro-preview","usage":{"input":8136,"output":35,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"totalTokens":8171,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1779705869962,"responseId":"ECgUaubaAYHG694PmJmegQo"}}
{"type":"message","id":"be762687","parentId":"fb93be46","timestamp":"2026-05-25T10:44:32.334Z","message":{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"exec_ts_ZTI0ODMwYTctNWNkNi00MmZlLTk5OGItZWU1MzllNzJiOWMz","toolName":"exec","content":[{"type":"text","text":"META\n- SEO Title: Bitcoin News: HYPE Surges 59% Amid ETF Outflows\n- Meta Description: Bitcoin news indicates a major shift in crypto capital. Investors pulled over $1 billion from Bitcoin ETFs last week, rotating funds into HYPE and XRP.\n- URL Slug: bitcoin-news-hype-surge-etf-outflows\n- Categories: Latest News, ETFs, Altcoins\n- Primary Keyword: Bitcoin News\n- Secondary Keywords: HYPE token, crypto ETF outflows, XRP ETF, Hyperliquid platform\n\n---\n\n# Bitcoin News: HYPE Token Funds See Big Gains as $1B Exits Major ETFs\n\nRecent Bitcoin news reveals a clear shift in how institutions are deploying capital across the cryptocurrency landscape. Investors withdrew more than $1 billion from Bitcoin exchange-traded funds over the past week, signaling a sharp reduction in appetite for broad, large-cap crypto exposure. Ether funds suffered a similar fate, losing over $215 million during the same period.\n\nThis mass exit does not indicate a complete withdrawal from digital assets. Capital is instead rotating toward emerging narratives and specific altcoins. New spot products tracking Hyperliquid’s HYPE token have successfully drawn approximately $72 million. Other targeted assets like XRP and Solana (SOL) are also seeing fresh inflows, capturing $22 million and $15.6 million, respectively, according to [recent flow data](https://www.coindesk.com/markets/2026/05/25/hype-funds-attract-millions-as-investors-dump-bitcoin-and-ether-etfs).\n\nThese movements suggest that investors are becoming highly selective. They are looking past the major benchmark assets to find concentrated value in specific ecosystems. The ability to easily access these niche markets through regulated spot products is accelerating the trend of capital rotation within the digital asset sector.\n\n## Institutions pivot to HYPE and altcoin ETFs\n\nThe divergence in fund flows highlights a maturing strategy among institutional players. Investors are increasingly willing to step away from benchmark assets like Bitcoin when momentum slows, opting instead to target high-growth protocols. The sudden influx into [HYPE spot products](https://www.coindesk.com/markets/2026/05/25/hype-funds-attract-millions-as-investors-dump-bitcoin-and-ether-etfs) issued by firms like Bitwise and 21Shares demonstrates this precise redeployment of capital.\n\nThis structural shift indicates a profound maturation of the digital asset space. Traders must actively adapt to these new market dynamics to maintain their edge and secure profits. Recognizing the signs of capital rotation early can provide significant trading advantages over those relying on outdated strategies. The market clearly rewards participants who pay close attention to specific protocol fundamentals rather than just passively following broad trends. These targeted investments require careful analysis of network activity, active user growth, and sustainable revenue metrics to identify the most promising opportunities.\n\n### The rise of Hyperliquid and HIP-3\n\nThe strong institutional demand for HYPE ETFs aligns directly with a massive rally in the underlying asset's price and explosive network activity. Hyperliquid has seen its native token jump from $38 to $63 over the past ten days alone. This represents a staggering 59% gain for the month, vastly outperforming Bitcoin's extremely modest 1% movement over the exact same timeframe.\n\nThe decentralized platform generated a massive $13.2 million in fees over a single seven-day stretch. This positions Hyperliquid firmly as the fifth-largest revenue generator in the entire crypto sector, trailing only stablecoin giants and popular launchpads. The platform's HIP-3 market is driving the vast majority of this financial success, consistently handling billions in open interest for perpetual futures tied directly to real-world assets.\n\n### Beyond traditional crypto derivatives\n\nHyperliquid is rapidly expanding its commercial footprint far beyond standard perpetual futures, putting it in direct, aggressive competition with traditional financial exchanges. The recent launch of HIP-4 outcome markets and pre-IPO trading capabilities has caught the undivided attention of major Wall Street firms. Analysts consistently note that equity perpetuals and prediction contracts are still in their very early stages, giving Hyperliquid a massive foundational advantage as it captures vital market share from established legacy players.\n\nRevenue projections for the dynamic platform remain exceptionally strong, heavily bolstered by a recent strategic integration agreement with Coinbase and Circle. The addition of USDC as a primary quote asset is widely expected to further streamline trading operations and attract even more massive waves of institutional liquidity to the expanding network.\n\n## The impact of targeted ETF approvals\n\nThe recent launch and approval of ETFs explicitly tied to specific altcoins like HYPE, XRP, and SOL is fundamentally altering core market dynamics. In the past, institutional capital primarily flowed into Bitcoin or Ethereum simply as proxy bets for the entire crypto sector. Now, sophisticated institutional investors can express highly specific, tactical views on individual protocols and their underlying technology stacks.\n\nThis structural shift perfectly explains the massive structural outflows from Bitcoin funds even as select altcoins experience rapid, sustained price appreciation. The broader market is definitively transitioning from a lengthy period of broad accumulation to one of highly selective capital allocation based squarely on fundamental metrics and actual revenue generation.\n\nThe availability of these targeted financial products allows for much more nuanced portfolio construction. Investors can hedge specific sector risks while aggressively pursuing yield in areas showing the strongest technical momentum and real-world adoption rates.\n\n### Analyzing the $1B Bitcoin outflow\n\nThe massive billion-dollar exodus from Bitcoin ETFs marks a significant and undeniable change in market sentiment following many long months of steady, continuous accumulation. While some of this specific capital is clearly rotating into higher-beta assets like HYPE, a notable portion may actually be moving to the sidelines as major investors reassess shifting macroeconomic conditions.\n\nThe cooling appetite for benchmark crypto exposure strongly suggests that Bitcoin may face severe near-term headwinds unless powerful new growth catalysts quickly emerge. However, the overall fundamental health of the broader digital asset ecosystem remains exceptionally robust, as clearly evidenced by the highly successful launch and immediate market traction of new, targeted altcoin ETF products.\n\n## Conclusion\n\nThe latest Bitcoin news paints a vivid picture of a rapidly evolving market where capital is actively seeking higher yields in emerging protocols. The massive outflows from Bitcoin and Ether ETFs, directly contrasted with surging interest in HYPE and XRP funds, prove that institutional investors are becoming increasingly selective with their capital deployment. As decentralized platforms like Hyperliquid aggressively expand their offerings to include real-world assets and pre-IPO markets, active traders should monitor these structural shifts extremely closely. The era of broad, indiscriminate crypto allocation appears to be rapidly fading, replaced firmly by a rigorous focus on fundamental strength and sustainable revenue growth.\n\n## FAQs\n\n**1. Why are investors pulling money from Bitcoin ETFs?**\n\nInvestors withdrew over $1 billion from Bitcoin ETFs last week due to waning appetite for broad, large-cap crypto exposure. The capital is not leaving the market entirely but is instead rotating into specific altcoins and high-growth protocols that offer better short-term momentum.\n\n**2. What is driving the price surge in the HYPE token?**\n\nHYPE has gained 59% this month due to strong institutional demand for new ETF products and surging network activity on the Hyperliquid platform. The decentralized exchange generated $13.2 million in fees in just seven days, driven by massive trading volumes in real-world asset perpetual futures.\n\n**3. Are other altcoins seeing ETF inflows?**\n\nYes, alongside the $72 million flowing into HYPE products, other targeted ETFs are attracting significant capital. XRP funds registered $22 million in inflows, while Solana (SOL) ETFs captured $15.6 million as investors diversify away from Bitcoin and Ether.\n\n**4. How is Hyperliquid competing with traditional markets?**\n\nHyperliquid is expanding beyond standard crypto derivatives into pre-IPO trading, prediction contracts, and tokenized real-world assets. Its HIP-3 and HIP-4 markets are handling billions in open interest for assets like oil, gold, and equity indexes, directly challenging traditional financial exchanges.\n\n**5. How will the Coinbase and Circle integration affect Hyperliquid?**\n\nThe recent agreement to integrate Circle's USDC as a quote asset on Hyperliquid is expected to significantly boost platform revenue and trading efficiency. This addition will streamline transactions and likely attract even more institutional liquidity to the decentralized exchange.\n\n**Sources:**\n- CoinDesk: https://www.coindesk.com/markets/2026/05/25/hype-funds-attract-millions-as-investors-dump-bitcoin-and-ether-etfs\n\n[Word Count: 1162]"}],"details":{"status":"completed","exitCode":0,"durationMs":16,"aggregated":"META\n- SEO Title: Bitcoin News: HYPE Surges 59% Amid ETF Outflows\n- Meta Description: Bitcoin news indicates a major shift in crypto capital. Investors pulled over $1 billion from Bitcoin ETFs last week, rotating funds 